//? Title: Shoelace area: edge sums and winding
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a closed polygonal boundary, compute its enclosed area from its corners
without triangulating its interior. Directed edge determinants produce a signed
area whose sign records winding for simple boundaries. We inspect actual
compensated edge sums, retain the square, triangle and concave chevron, and test
translation and regular-polygon limits.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Green's theorem gives one half of the boundary integral \(x\,dy-y\,dx\).
Parametrizing one straight edge reduces its contribution to the determinant of
its endpoints:

$$ A_s=\frac12\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}(x_i y_{i+1}-x_{i+1}y_i),\qquad v_n=v_0.$$
\alttext(Signed area is half the sum of determinants around the implicitly closed boundary.)

Reversing the traversal reverses every edge and negates the sum. For a simple
counterclockwise boundary the sign is positive; absolute value is its enclosed
area. Self-crossings give algebraic area, not union area: oppositely wound lobes
may cancel. Repeated first/last corners are permitted.

The native producer subtracts the first corner before evaluating determinants
and uses compensated long-double summation. Translation invariance cancels
analytically; anchoring reduces avoidable floating-point cancellation. It does
not make an exact predicate, and the final double area can underflow. The edge
table is the actual anchored computation, starting with the closing
last-to-first edge. Time is linear, with constant working storage beyond
optional linear history.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/shoelace_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/random/quality.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/shoelace.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = dm::geometry::shoelace_trace({{0, 0}, {4, 0}, {0, 3}});
    std::print("| from | to | anchored determinant | signed partial area |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: " "|\n");
    for (const auto &e : r.edges)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", e.from, e.to, e.determinant,
                   e.signed_partial_area);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Triangle (0,0), (4,0), (0,3)");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual edge terms", r.edge_terms));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Signed area {}", r.signed_area));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/shoelace_tiny.svg");
}
```
